# Transaction and Audit Events

## Purpose

This chapter defines the canonical events governing administrative operations, system transactions, and immutable audit history.

Unlike Ownership, Financial, Draft, Trade, and Waiver events, which describe changes to League assets, Transaction and Audit events describe **how those changes were coordinated, validated, recorded, and preserved**.

Every state-changing operation performed within Legacy belongs to a Transaction.

Every Transaction generates an immutable Audit history.

Together they provide complete traceability, reproducibility, and accountability.

---

# Guiding Principle

Business Events describe League state.

Transactions coordinate Business Events.

Audit Events preserve Business Events forever.

Nothing that changes League state exists without an auditable transaction.

---

# Transaction Model

```text
Command
      │
creates
      ▼
Transaction
      │
coordinates
      ▼
Business Events
      │
recorded as
      ▼
Audit Events
```

Transactions do not own League assets.

Transactions coordinate the execution of asset changes.

Audit Events preserve historical truth.

---

# Transaction Lifecycle

```text
TransactionStarted
        │
        ▼
TransactionValidated
        │
        ▼
Business Events Emitted
        │
        ▼
TransactionCommitted
```

Alternative paths

```text
TransactionStarted

↓

TransactionFailed
```

or

```text
TransactionStarted

↓

TransactionRolledBack
```

---

# Audit Lifecycle

```text
Business Event

↓

AuditEventRecorded

↓

AuditArchived
```

Historical events are never deleted.

---

# Scope

This chapter defines:

- TransactionStarted
- TransactionValidated
- TransactionCommitted
- TransactionFailed
- TransactionRolledBack

- AuditEventRecorded
- AuditEventCorrected
- AuditArchived

- AdministrativeOverrideApplied
- AdministrativeCorrectionPerformed

---

# General Invariants

Every Transaction shall satisfy:

1. Every Transaction has a unique identifier.
2. Every Business Event belongs to exactly one Transaction.
3. Transactions execute atomically.
4. Transactions are immutable after commitment.
5. Failed Transactions shall not modify League state.
6. Audit Events are append-only.
7. Historical records shall never be destroyed.
8. Every administrative action must generate an Audit Event.
9. Every correction preserves historical truth.
10. Every event remains replayable.

---

# TransactionStarted

## Purpose

Represents the beginning of a coordinated state-changing operation.

### Trigger

A Command begins execution.

### Business Impact

• Creates Transaction context

• Allocates transaction identifier

• Begins validation pipeline

---

# TransactionValidated

## Purpose

Represents successful validation prior to execution.

### Business Impact

• Confirms legality

• Locks execution state

• Authorizes event emission

---

# TransactionCommitted

## Purpose

Represents successful completion of every operation within the Transaction.

### Business Impact

• Finalizes Business Events

• Persists deterministic state

• Enables replay

• Enables audit

### AI Interpretation

The AI recognizes every committed Transaction as authoritative League history.

---

# TransactionFailed

## Purpose

Represents unsuccessful execution.

### Business Impact

• No League state changes

• Records failure reason

• Preserves audit trail

---

# TransactionRolledBack

## Purpose

Represents reversal of a partially executed Transaction.

### Business Impact

• Restores previous League state

• Prevents inconsistent ownership

• Preserves failure history

---

# AuditEventRecorded

## Purpose

Creates an immutable historical record of a Business Event.

### Business Impact

• Preserves historical truth

• Enables deterministic replay

• Enables forensic analysis

• Enables compliance

---

# AuditEventCorrected

## Purpose

Represents an administrative correction to historical metadata without deleting original records.

### Business Impact

• Preserves original history

• Appends correction record

• Maintains historical integrity

---

# AuditArchived

## Purpose

Represents archival of historical Audit records while preserving replay capability.

### Business Impact

• Reduces operational storage

• Maintains historical accessibility

• Preserves replay integrity

---

# AdministrativeOverrideApplied

## Purpose

Represents an authorized manual override performed by League administration.

Examples include:

- Force approving a Trade
- Adjusting Draft Order
- Correcting Ownership
- Correcting Contracts
- Restoring Franchise state

### Business Impact

• Alters deterministic League state

• Generates mandatory Audit record

• Preserves administrator identity

• Records override reason

### AI Interpretation

The AI shall recognize administrative overrides as authoritative even when they supersede normal business rules.

---

# AdministrativeCorrectionPerformed

## Purpose

Represents correction of erroneous historical state while preserving complete event history.

### Business Impact

• Corrects League state

• Preserves audit history

• Records correction chain

---

# Workflow Examples

## Successful Transaction

```text
Command

↓

TransactionStarted

↓

TransactionValidated

↓

Business Events

↓

TransactionCommitted

↓

AuditEventRecorded
```

---

## Failed Transaction

```text
Command

↓

TransactionStarted

↓

TransactionFailed
```

---

## Administrative Override

```text
AdministrativeOverrideApplied

↓

Business Events

↓

AuditEventRecorded
```

---

## Historical Correction

```text
AdministrativeCorrectionPerformed

↓

AuditEventCorrected
```

---

# Relationship to Other Chapters

Every Business Event documented within this Event Catalog executes inside a Transaction.

Examples include:

```text
TradeExecuted

ContractCreated

RosterAssignmentTransferred

DraftSelectionMade

FreeAgentSigned

LeagueMembershipActivated
```

These Business Events define League state.

Transactions coordinate their execution.

Audit Events permanently preserve their history.

---

# AI Interpretation

Transaction and Audit events provide authoritative evidence regarding:

- Event ordering

- Historical replay

- Administrative actions

- Transaction success

- Transaction failure

- Data lineage

- League integrity

- System accountability

The AI shall treat committed Transactions as the canonical ordering mechanism for every deterministic calculation performed within Legacy.

---

# Validation Checklist

Before publishing Transaction and Audit events verify:

✓ Transaction identifier unique

✓ Event ordering deterministic

✓ Transaction atomicity preserved

✓ Failed Transactions isolated

✓ Rollback integrity maintained

✓ Audit history append-only

✓ Administrative actions audited

✓ Replay deterministic

✓ Historical truth preserved

✓ No destructive corrections performed

---

# Final Principle

Transactions are the execution engine of Legacy.

Business Events describe what changed.

Transactions guarantee those changes occur safely, atomically, and deterministically.

Audit Events guarantee those changes remain permanently observable.

Together they form the foundation of Legacy's event-sourced architecture, ensuring every League can be reconstructed, verified, and explained from immutable historical evidence.
